#strong[Directional sensitivity (H1).] All four configurations moved in the annotated direction, and for all four the adjusted bound exceeds the 5-point SESOI: #emph[s] = 17.6, 22.0, 26.6 and 28.3 points. The difference between the posteriors after a strengthener and after a weakener, D, is 35.2, 44.0, 53.3 and 56.6 points. Zero movement under stated presentation was less common than in the local models (6.7%–15.0% of update conditions, against 16%–29%).

#strong[Reporting wording (H2).] Recasting the update as a report reduced the directional movement in three configurations (3.1, 4.1 and 4.1 points; direction confirmed, point estimates below the SESOI); for gpt-oss-120b the difference was 0.9 points and the direction was not established. As in the original three configurations, the reversal rate was higher under reported than under stated presentation in all four (0.163 → 0.192, 0.079 → 0.125, 0.062 → 0.092 and 0.050 → 0.079), so under the pre-specified rule the conclusion is again worded as "reporting shifts the direction of movement". By update type (descriptive; @tab-type, lower four rows), the reduction lay mainly in strengtheners for gpt-oss-120b (+17.8 → +16.1; weakeners −17.3 → −17.2), mainly in weakeners for claude-sonnet-5.5 (−18.6 → −13.9) and gemini-3.8-flash (low) (−26.4 → −19.6), and was spread over both types for gemini-3.8-flash (high) (+27.9 → +24.9; −28.7 → −23.5).

#strong[Multi-estimate elicitation (H4a, H4b).] The weighted endpoint rate under direct elicitation was 14.4%, 24.0%, 38.8% and 32.3%, and under multi-estimate elicitation 4.2%, 17.5%, 22.5% and 23.3%. The reduction passed Holm correction in all four configurations; only for gemini-3.8-flash (low) does the adjusted bound exceed the 10-point SESOI. The change in directional sensitivity was small: −2.3, +0.5, −2.5 and −3.7 points (direction confirmed in three configurations, point estimates below the SESOI; not established for claude-sonnet-5.5). Across candidate-level readings (@tab-readings, lower four rows), all four extension configurations show the ordering top-weighted greater than weighted mean, which is greater than unweighted mean. The pattern seen in the local models recurs on a smaller scale: under multi-estimate elicitation, zero movement almost disappears (at most 3.3% of update conditions), the reversal rate rises (pooled: 0.163 → 0.212, 0.079 → 0.117, 0.062 → 0.133, 0.050 → 0.113), and the pooled agreement rate does not fall (0.746 → 0.783, 0.846 → 0.875, 0.804 → 0.846, 0.846 → 0.887). The weighted standard deviation of the five candidates under stated presentation was 14.0 points for gpt-oss-120b and 6.3–7.5 points for the other three; every multi-estimate response had weights summing to a value in \[0.95, 1.05\].

#strong[Robustness (pre-specified).] Excluding the three Block-A pairs with quoted-speech updates (117 pairs remaining) leaves every conclusion unchanged (e.g., H2: 0.85, 3.07, 4.16 and 4.19 points). Stratified by material source, H2 was larger on δ-SNLI dev items for two configurations and on test items for the other two, unlike the original three configurations, where it was larger on dev items in all three. Between-call variability was small except for gpt-oss-120b: on the repeat subset, the mean absolute difference in #emph[s] between two calls of the same prompt was 5.9, 1.4, 2.3 and 1.4 points, against between-item standard deviations of 17.5, 14.5, 15.5 and 14.7 points.

#strong[Reasoning effort (descriptive only).] The two gemini-3.8-flash configurations differ only in reasoning effort. On the common 120 pairs, high minus low effort was +1.7 points for H1 (descriptive 95% interval \[0.3, 3.2\]), 0.0 for H2 (\[−1.9, 2.0\]), −0.073 for H4a (\[−0.110, −0.037\]) and −1.3 for H4b (\[−2.7, 0.1\]). These contrasts are not part of any Holm family.

== Candidate spread (exploratory, not pre-specified)

Under multi-estimate elicitation each response gives five candidate probabilities with weights, so it also carries a spread: the weighted standard deviation of the candidates (weights normalized to sum to 1; SD = √Σ wᵢ(xᵢ − m)², where m is the weighted mean). We asked three questions after seeing the formal results: does the spread shrink after an update, is it larger after a report than after a stated update, and do items with a more dispersed prior move more? Intervals are cluster-bootstrap 95% intervals (B = 2,000, seed 20261500); none of these analyses enters a Holm family. Effective complete-pair counts are n = 240 for Qwen3 (thinking off), n = 236–238 for OLMo-2, n = 120 Block-A pairs each for codex and the four dnli-agy configurations, and n = 115–120 for the eight dnli-api configurations (@tab-api-formal). Spearman ρ assigns average ranks to ties and is undefined if either variable has zero variance across pairs; its bootstrap interval is undefined if any resample is degenerate (0 undefined resamples occurred across all seven configurations).

